Every one of the 256 patterns is a positive number;
there is no minus sign anywhere yet. To make room for negatives we sacrifice the <b>top
(leftmost) bit</b> and let it carry the sign: a top bit of 0 means positive.

(1111 1011 + 0000 0101 = 1&nbsp;0000 0000;
throw away the carry and you have 0000 0000, which is zero.) A number plus its negative gives
zero, which is exactly what a negative should do. The scheme really works.

Fourth, and the one the exam cares about,
<b>control transfer</b>: JUMP, BRANCH, CALL. Normally instructions run one after another in order;
these <b>change that order</b>.
